{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_12",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_12_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_19",
      "quote": "We summarised the effect of interventions on dichotomous outcomes using the odds ratio and corresponding 95% credible interval. For continuous outcomes, we used the mean difference ... For time to symptom resolution, we first performed the analyses using the relative effect measure ratio of means ... before calculating the mean difference in days",
      "reason": "The Summary Measures section explicitly specifies effect measures for each outcome type (odds ratio, mean difference, ratio of means)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_12_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_22",
      "quote": "To evaluate certainty of no benefit (or no effect), we used a 2% risk difference threshold of the 95% credible interval for mortality and mechanical ventilation. In other words, if the entire 95% credible interval was within 2% of the null effect, we would not rate down for imprecision. We decided on this preliminary threshold based on a survey of the authors.",
      "reason": "A threshold (2% risk difference) with rationale is reported, but only for imprecision judgments on two outcomes in the certainty assessment; general thresholds for interpreting effect sizes are not comprehensively reported, though a minimally contextualised framework with null effect as threshold of importance is mentioned."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_12_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_23",
      "quote": "To facilitate interpretation of the results, we calculated absolute effects for outcomes in which the summary measure was an odds ratio or ratio of means. ... We calculated absolute effects using the transitive risks model 33 using R2jags package in R.",
      "reason": "Re-expression of results as absolute effects from relative measures (odds ratio, ratio of means) is described, including the method (transitive risks model) and data sources for baseline risks; text_19 also describes converting ratio of means to mean difference."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_12_e4",
